L000095	
L000096	\date{\today}
L000097	\begin{abstract}
L000098	We study Gutzwiller-projected wavefunctions for triangular-lattice U(1) Dirac spin liquids in a Zeeman field, where we allow the U(1) gauge field to develop a gauge flux, resulting in (spin-split) spinon Landau levels. We find that at a given magnetization, the optimal candidate state has a finite flux chosen such that the spinon filling lies in a $|C|=1$ Landau-level gap: it gives the lowest variational energy and the smallest energy variance within our correlation-matrix reconstruction for local Heisenberg-type models.
L000099	By symmetry, we argue that the finite gauge flux results in a non-zero (staggered) scalar spin chirality, as also numerically observed, and further find that the $|C|=1$ state exhibits dominant quasi-long-ranged $120^\circ$ magnetic correlations.
L000100	Studying the next-to-optimal wavefunction with a $|C|=2$ Landau-level gap, we observe unusual spin-nematic correlations. Our results may provide guidance for analyzing the magnetic-field response of DSL candidate materials and offer numerical diagnostics that can connect to the underlying theory of spinons coupled to an emergent U(1) gauge field.
L000101	
L000102	
L000103	\end{abstract}
L000104	\maketitle
L000105	
L000106	{\color{Blue4}\textit{Introduction.---}}Quantum spin liquids (QSLs) are exotic phases of quantum magnets which exhibit long-ranged entanglement and fractionalized excitations, described by the deconfined phases of emergent gauge theories~\cite{Savary_2017,annurev:/content/journals/10.1146/annurev-conmatphys-031218-013401}.
L000107	As prototypical examples of strongly-correlated matter beyond Landau’s symmetry-breaking framework, they have attracted wide interest, both due to their fundamental properties, but also possible connections to high-$T_c$ superconductivity, quantum magnetism, and potential applications in quantum information science.
L000108	While a number of candidate materials have been investigated in recent years, and complementary progress has also come from programmable quantum simulators (e.g., Ref.~\onlinecite{bornet2026diracspinliquidcandidate}), an unambiguous identification and characterization of a QSL is impeded by a scarcity of experimental signatures that can be directly attributed to the presence of an emergent gauge field.
L000109	
L000110	A magnetic field is a natural, experimentally relevant perturbation; the characteristic response of a quantum magnet to a Zeeman field may reveal properties of the zero-field ground state.
L000111	Thus, motivated by recent experiments suggesting a quantum-disordered ground state in triangular-lattice antiferromagnets~\cite{Xie23,PhysRevLett.133.266703,Scheie2024,Scheie2024b,Wu2025}, we here investigate the field-induced states emerging from a U(1) Dirac spin liquid (DSL) as a candidate ground state~\cite{PhysRevB.92.140403,PhysRevB.93.144411,Hu19,Song2019,Sherman2023,Drescher2023}.
L000112	In the U(1) DSL, gapless fermionic spinons couple to an emergent U(1) gauge field. At low energies, this state is described by compact quantum electrodynamics (QED$_3$) in 2+1 dims \cite{Hermele2005,Song2019}.
L000113	
L000114	Previous mean-field considerations by Ran \textit{et al.}~\cite{PhysRevLett.102.047205} indicate that upon applying a Zeeman field the emergent U(1) gauge field develops a finite gauge flux.
L000115	As a consequence, the spinon Dirac cones give way to relativistic Landau levels, with a finite magnetization given by the spin polarization of spinons in the half-filled 0th Landau level. Recent Quantum Monte Carlo simulations of an effectively \emph{non-compact} version of the QED$_3$ low-energy field theory \cite{chen2025emergent} provide evidence that the field-induced flux phase persists in the presence of gauge fluctuations.
L000116	
L000117	A powerful tool to construct a microscopic wavefunction associated with an (effective) parton description is the Gutzwiller projection technique: here, one starts from a free mean-field wavefunction for fermionic partons and explicitly projects to the physical $S=1/2$ Hilbert space. The resulting wavefunction is in general highly non-trivial, but, crucially, observables and matrix elements can be evaluated efficiently using variational Monte Carlo (VMC) techniques~\cite{BeccaSorella2017}. While Gutzwiller-projected wavefunctions may yield competitive variational energies \cite{PhysRevB.93.144411, iqbal21,Wietek2024} and even constitute exact ground states \cite{shastry88}, there is little systematic understanding of the physical properties and signatures of the projected wavefunction for a given spinon ansatz state.
L000118	
L000119	Given the field-induced ``chiral flux'' state of QED$_3$, in this Letter, we systematically construct and characterize corresponding microscopic Gutzwiller-projected wavefunctions for frustrated quantum magnets.
L000120	Specifically, we consider a class of wavefunctions describing Dirac spinons on the triangular lattice in the presence of an additional uniform gauge flux $\phi$, which we take as a variational parameter. We find a finite optimal gauge flux $\phi$ for a given magnetization $m \neq 0$. The corresponding projected wavefunction exhibits a \emph{quasi}-long-ranged 120$^\circ$ order and features a finite scalar spin chirality as an order parameter sensitive to a non-zero gauge flux.
L000121	
L000122	
L000123	{\color{Blue4}\textit{Choice of optimal flux.---}}
L000124	
L000125	We first construct Gutzwiller-projected wavefunctions via an auxiliary free-fermion spinon Hamiltonian and find the optimal value of the flux per primitive rhombic unit cell $\phi$ (we provide details on Gutzwiller-projected wavefunctions in the End Matter).
L000126	For a Heisenberg-type system of $S=1/2$ in a magnetic field, the total magnetization is a good quantum number: we consider variational states at a fixed magnetization density $m = (N S)^{-1} \sum_i S^z_i$ where $N$ is the number of sites.
L000127	Below, for simplicity, we focus on representative rational magnetizations, in particular $m=1/3$ and $m=2/3$. We emphasize that the construction of a finite-flux Gutzwiller wavefunction proceeds analogously for \emph{any} magnetization $m$ (with an appropriate choice of flux per unit cell).
L000128	
L000129	\begin{figure}[t]
L000130	    \centering
L000131	    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{energy.pdf}
L000132	    \caption{
L000133	    (a) Hofstadter-butterfly spectrum of the staggered-$\pi$ triangular-lattice spinon Hamiltonian on an $18\times18$ cluster: single-particle energies $\epsilon$ vs flux density $\phi/2\pi$.
L000134	    Labels mark gap Chern numbers;
L000135	    symbols mark the spin-up/down Fermi levels in the $C=\pm1$ gaps selected by the energy minima in (b,d).
L000136	(b,d) Variational energies per site of Eq.~\ref{eq:h-nn} vs $\phi$
L000137	at fixed $m = 1/3$ and $2/3$.
L000138	Since we compare states at a fixed magnetization, the Zeeman energy $\sim h m$ contributes an overall (global) energy shift and is therefore omitted.
L000139	Symbols denote the linear system size ($N_x=N_y$).
L000140	Vertical dotted lines mark gap-opening fluxes with the smallest nonzero $|C|$ (notably $|C|=1$).
L000141	For $N_x<30$ we sample all closed-shell commensurate fluxes, whereas for $N_x\ge 30$ we keep the closed-shell $\phi=0$ reference and finite-$\phi$ fluxes with spin-resolved Fermi levels in well-defined Chern gaps.
L000142	(c) $\lambda_{\min}$ vs maximum coupling range for the Fermi-pocket state and the $|C|=1,2$ Landau-level states. Dotted (solid) lines correspond to $6\times6$ ($12\times12$) lattices.
L000143	Inset: minimal fluctuation eigenvalue $\lambda_{\min}$ for range-$2$ couplings.
L000144	}
L000145	    \label{fig:energy}
L000146	\end{figure}
L000147	
L000148	For $m=0$, the staggered-$\pi$ flux ansatz with zero net flux provides a competitive approximation to the ground state of the triangular-lattice $J_1$-$J_2$ model, even in the absence of (variational) tuning parameters~\cite{PhysRevB.93.144411,PhysRevX.9.031026,Wietek2024,Sasank2025}.
L000149	This flux-free state can be extended to support a finite magnetization $m>0$ if the spin-up and spin-down Fermi levels are moved above and below the spinon Dirac nodes, respectively, resulting in \textit{Fermi pockets}.
L000150	However, upon adding a uniform flux, \emph{Chern gaps} may open in the spinon spectrum -- analogous to the gaps between Landau levels -- which lower the system's energy, as found in mean-field and Quantum Monte Carlo studies of the QED$_3$~\cite{PhysRevLett.102.047205,chen2025emergent}.
L000151	A priori, the value of $\phi$ is arbitrary and defines a one-parameter family of wavefunctions at fixed $m$. What is the (energetically) optimal choice?
L000152	To this end, we first investigate the spectrum of the auxiliary spinon Hamiltonian as a function of $\phi$, shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:energy}(a).
L000153	The largest topological gaps have $|C| = 1$, suggesting that the optimal flux is such that the spin-up Fermi level lies in a $C=+1$ gap and the spin-down Fermi level in the symmetric $C=-1$ gap about $\epsilon=0$.
L000154	
L000155	To go beyond such a mean-field analysis, we now investigate which choice of $\phi$ minimizes the variational energy of the corresponding \emph{Gutzwiller-projected} wavefunctions with respect to the nearest-neighbour Heisenberg model
L000156	\begin{equation} \label{eq:h-nn}
L000157	H = J_{\mathrm{NN}} \sum_{\langle i,j \rangle} \vec{S}_i \cdot \vec{S}_j.
L000158	\end{equation}
L000159	At fixed $m$, a uniform Zeeman term only adds a constant and is omitted from Eq.~(1).
L000160	The variational energies in Figs.~\ref{fig:energy}(b,d) have strong finite-size effects when the Fermi level lies outside a well-defined gap or the gap is small.
L000161	The ``Fermi-pocket'' state ($\phi=0$) and its immediate low-flux vicinity have substantially higher energy than states with a sizable flux, where the spinon Fermi level lies in a Chern gap and the spinons form a gapped ``Landau-level'' state.
L000162	Within statistical and finite-size uncertainties, the lowest variational energies occur when the Fermi level lies in the largest $|C|=1$ gap, making these $|C|=1$ flux states the energetically most favorable flux configurations at the Gutzwiller-projected level.
L000163	This general behavior is robust when including longer-ranged interactions.
L000164	In particular, at the strongly frustrated point $J_{\mathrm{NNN}}/J_{\mathrm{NN}}=1/8$ a similar flux dependence is observed (see Fig.~S2 in \cite{SuppMat}).
L000165	
L000166	{\color{Blue4}\textit{Parent Hamiltonian search.---}}
L000167	We further test whether these states admit (quasi-)local parent Hamiltonians~\cite{Qi2019determininglocal}
L000168	\begin{equation}
L000169	H = J_0 \sum_{m=1}^{5} \gamma_m \sum_{\langle ij\rangle_m} \vec S_i \!\cdot\! \vec S_j,
L000170	\qquad \lVert \gamma \rVert_2 = 1
L000171	\end{equation}
L000172	i.e., Heisenberg exchanges up to $5$th neighbors.
L000173	For a given \(\ket{\psi}\), we vary $\gamma$ to minimize the energy variance (see Sec.~VII in \cite{SuppMat}), and use the minimal energy variance per site $J_0^2\lambda_{\min}$ as a relative measure of how closely \(\ket{\psi}\) can be approximated by a local Heisenberg-type Hamiltonian.
L000174	
L000175	The inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:energy}(c) shows that, for range-$2$ couplings (nearest- and next-nearest-neighbor), the Fermi-pocket state and the $|C|=2$ Landau-level state have larger $\lambda_{\mathrm{min}}$ than the $|C|=1$ state (which also has the lowest variational energy);
L000176	$\lambda_{\mathrm{min}}$ further decreases with magnetization (consistent with suppressed quantum fluctuations).
L000177	The reconstructed Hamiltonian at $\lambda_{\mathrm{min}}$ estimates the optimal coupling profile;
L000178	for the $m=0$ state it yields an optimal $J_{\mathrm{NNN}}/J_{\mathrm{NN}}$ (Fig.~S10 in \cite{SuppMat}) that broadly agrees with the spin-liquid window from previous VMC, density matrix renormalization group, and coupled cluster method studies~\cite{PhysRevB.93.144411,PhysRevB.92.041105,PhysRevB.92.140403,PhysRevB.91.014426}.
L000179	Extending the allowed coupling range reduces $\lambda_{\min}$ for all states [Fig.~\ref{fig:energy}(c)],
L000180	but within this restricted setting the $|C|=1$ state remains the most practical to realize with a quasi-local Heisenberg-type Hamiltonian.
L000181	
L000182	{\color{Blue4}\textit{In-plane N\'eel Order.---}}
L000183	
L000184	We now investigate experimentally accessible spin correlations in the above-constructed states with a finite field-induced gauge flux $\phi$.
L000185	In the parton picture, these states can be viewed as a quantum Hall phase of the spinons in an emergent magnetic field, with opposite Hall conductivities of $\uparrow$- and $\downarrow$-spinons due to their differential occupation of the spin-split Landau levels. Inserting an additional quantum of this emergent flux can be thought of as a defect of the emergent gauge field in space-time (a ``monopole''), which binds finite spin $\Delta S^z$~\cite{PhysRevLett.102.047205,chen2025emergent,Paoletti2025}.
L000186	In a low-energy field theory description, this is reflected by joint monopole-insertion and spin flip $\langle M_{2\pi}^\dagger S^+ \rangle \neq 0$ as a ``hidden'' order parameter of the flux state~\cite{chen2025emergent}.
L000187	
L000188	\begin{figure}[t]
L000189	    \centering
L000190	    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{contrastq.pdf}
L000191	    \caption{
L000192	(a) Monopole matrix element $\langle S_i^+\rangle_{\text{mono}}$ for $|C|=1$ at $m=2/3$, where the color encodes the phase, shown relative to site $(0,0)$, and the marker size encodes the magnitude.
L000193	(b,d,f) Real-space transverse spin correlator $C_{ij}^{\perp}$ on $12\times 12$ lattices with reference site at $(0,0)$.
L000194	The color scale is clipped to $[-0.15,0.15]$, so $C_{00}^{\perp}=0.5$ appears saturated.
L000195	(c,e,g) Corresponding structure factor $C^{\perp}(\mathbf{q})$ on $18\times 18$ lattices.
L000196	(b,c) use the nearest available $m$ to $1/3$; the slight mismatch reflects finite-size closed-shell constraints at different lattice sizes.
L000197	    }
L000198	    \label{fig:order_profile}
L000199	\end{figure}
L000200	
L000201	On a finite torus, a monopole excitation can be constructed by spreading a $2 \pi$ flux across the lattice~\cite{Wietek2024,Sasank2025}, so that we may evaluate the order parameter as
L000202	\begin{equation} \label{eq:msplus}
L000203	    \langle S_i^+\rangle_{\text{mono}} \equiv \langle M_{2\pi}^\dagger S^+_i \rangle \equiv \frac{\langle n+1 \vert P\, S_i^+\, P \vert n \rangle}{\langle n \vert P \vert n \rangle} ,
L000204	\end{equation}
L000205	where $|n\rangle$ is the mean-field Landau-level state with $n$ flux quanta and $P$ the Gutzwiller projection.
L000206	We normalize by $\langle n|P|n\rangle$ in VMC.
L000207	For $|C|=1$, inserting one additional flux quantum pumps an additional state in the $\uparrow$- and $\downarrow$-spinon bands with $C= \pm1$, respectively. Thus, the net spin pumped is $\Delta S^z=1$, so $P \vert n \rangle$  and $P \vert n+1 \rangle$ must lie in adjacent $S^z$ sectors and $\langle S_i^+\rangle_{\text{mono}}$ can be nonzero.
L000208	While in this case, Eq.~\eqref{eq:msplus} behaves as an effective transverse spin-$1$ order-parameter, for $|C|>1$, a monopole binds a spin quantum number of $\Delta S^z =|C| > 1$
L000209	and $\langle S_i^+\rangle_{\text{mono}}$ vanishes.
L000210	The phase of $\langle S_i^+\rangle_{\text{mono}}$ for $|C|=1$ forms a clear three-sublattice $120^\circ$ pattern [Fig.~\ref{fig:order_profile}(a)], characteristic of in-plane N\'eel order on the triangular lattice.
L000211	
L000212	While the monopole matrix element serves as a ``hidden'' order parameter, we examine the real-space transverse spin correlator and its momentum-space structure factor as experimentally accessible observables:
L000213	\begin{align}
L000214	C_{ij}^{\perp} &\equiv\langle S_i^xS_j^x + S_i^yS_j^y \rangle =  \frac{1}{2}\langle S_i^+S_j^- + S_i^-S_j^+ \rangle \\
L000215	C^{\perp} (\mathbf{q})&= \frac{1}{N}\sum_{ij} e^{i\mathbf{q}\cdot(\mathbf{r}_i-\mathbf{r}_j)} C_{ij}^{\perp}.
L000216	\end{align}
L000217	Figures~\ref{fig:order_profile}(b,c) show that in the magnetized ``Fermi-pocket'' state $C_{ij}^{\perp}$ is consistent with collinear antiferromagnetic stripe correlations, and $C^{\perp}(\mathbf{q})$ peaks at the $M$ points.
L000218	For flux states at $m=1/3$ [close to $m$ in (b,c), cf. Fig.~S1 in \cite{SuppMat}], we focus on the smallest Chern numbers $|C|=1,2$, corresponding to the two largest spinon gaps:
L000219	In (d-g), $C_{ij}^{\perp}$ shows a pattern consistent with $120^\circ$ N\'eel order on the triangular lattice, and $C^{\perp}(\mathbf{q})$ develops peaks at the $K$ points, independent of the system's magnetization (for $|C|=1$, see Fig.~\ref{fig:em_order_profile} in the End Matter).
L000220	Thus, the Fermi-pocket and Landau-level states are distinguished by the leading transverse spin correlations, and this qualitative difference in the spin structure factor could provide an experimentally accessible signature of the Landau-level state.
L000221	Note that we observe $K$-point in-plane correlations for both $|C|=1$ and $2$.
L000222	However, the $|C|=2$ states show considerably weaker correlations than $|C|=1$ (see also Fig.~S5 in \cite{SuppMat}),
L000223	consistent with the vanishing of the ``hidden'' order parameter $\langle S^+_i \rangle_\mathrm{mono}$.
L000224	
L000225	\begin{figure}[t]
L000226	    \centering
L000227	    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{inplanescaling.pdf}
L000228	    \caption{
L000229	    Finite-size scaling of transverse spin order.
L000230	(a) $120^\circ$ order normalized structure factor $C^{\perp}(\mathbf{q}=K)/N$ versus $N^{-1/2}$.
L000231	(b) Site-averaged monopole matrix element $\overline{|\langle S_i^+\rangle_{\text{mono}}|}$ versus $N^{-1/4}$.
L000232	Insets show the same data plotted against $N^{-1}$.
L000233	Dashed lines are linear fits using $N_x=N_y\ge 12$.
L000234	    }
L000235	    \label{fig:inplane_Scaling}
L000236	\end{figure}
L000237	
L000238	To probe the nature of in-plane spin correlations at long distances, we perform a finite-size scaling of the structure factor $C^{\perp} (\mathbf{q})$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:inplane_Scaling}(a)].
L000239	In particular, a divergence $C^\perp(\mathbf{q})\sim N$  at some $\mathbf{q}$, i.e. a Bragg peak, will signal the formation of off-diagonal long-range order, $\lim_{|i-j|\to \infty } C_{ij}^\perp > 0$ in the thermodynamic limit, i.e. spontaneous in-plane magnetic ordering.
L000240	
L000241	The inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:inplane_Scaling}(a) shows that for $|C|=2$, $C^{\perp} (\mathbf{q}=K)/N$
L000242	is roughly linear in $N^{-1}$ and extrapolates to zero in the thermodynamic limit, consistent with real-space summable (short-ranged) transverse correlations
L000243	(e.g., decaying exponentially or as $1/r^p$ with $p>2$).
L000244	For $|C|=1$ the data deviate from a simple $1/N$ scaling: a linear fit in $N^{-1}$ does not capture the largest system sizes and, if extrapolated, would give an apparent positive intercept, indicating that $C^{\perp} (\mathbf{q}=K)/N$ decreases more slowly than $N^{-1}$.
L000245	In the main panel of Fig.~\ref{fig:inplane_Scaling}(a), the $|C|=1$ points are much closer to straight lines when plotted versus $N^{-1/2}$, though extrapolation gives a slightly negative intercept.
L000246	This suggests an approximate scaling $C^{\perp} (\mathbf{q}=K)/N \propto N^{-1/2}$, compatible with a slow algebraic decay $1/r^p$ with $p\approx 1$
L000247	rather than a strictly finite plateau. (See also Fig.~S3 in \cite{SuppMat} for the logarithmic-scale analysis.)
L000248	This indicates
L000249	more extended correlations for $|C|=1$ than for $|C|=2$.
L000250	Indeed, a recent study has argued (using an effective description in terms of Laughlin wavefunctions) that Gutzwiller-projecting a quantum spin Hall state yields algebraic $1/r$ pair correlations \cite{nn2m-w4vk}.
L000251	
L000252	It is interesting to compare our finite-size scaling of the structure factor with that of the hidden order parameter $\langle S^+_i \rangle_\mathrm{mono} = \langle M_{2\pi}^\dagger S^+_i \rangle$.
L000253	In the thermodynamic limit, for the $|C|=1$ state, one may expect this to be related to in-plane long-range order in the spin structure factor: upon inserting a resolution of identity (over all flux sectors) and truncating,  $\lim_{|i-j| \to \infty} \langle S^+_i S^-_j +\mathrm{h.c.}\rangle \approx \langle  S^+_i M_{2\pi}^\dagger \rangle \langle M_{2\pi} S^-_j \rangle +\mathrm{h.c.}$
L000254	Inserting a single flux breaks translational symmetry,
L000255	resulting in site-dependent $\langle S_i^+\rangle_{\text{mono}}$ [Fig.~\ref{fig:order_profile}(a)]. Because the magnitude varies weakly, we use the site-averaged $\overline{|\langle S_i^+\rangle_{\text{mono}}|}$ and show its finite-size scaling in Fig.~\ref{fig:inplane_Scaling}(b).
L000256	Notably, in the thermodynamic limit, $\overline{|\langle S_i^+\rangle_{\text{mono}}|} \to 0$.
L000257	The near-linearity versus $N^{-1/4}$
L000258	is consistent with trends in (a).
L000259	We have also checked the effect of changing the normalization convention to the symmetric form
L000260	$\sqrt{\langle n|P|n\rangle\langle n+1|P|n+1\rangle}$;
L000261	this changes only the overall magnitude, but not the finite-size scaling (see Fig.~S4 in \cite{SuppMat}).
L000262	Together,
L000263	this supports more long-ranged, power-law in-plane correlations for $|C|=1$ than for $|C|=2$,
L000264	but without conclusive evidence for genuine long-range order in the thermodynamic limit.
L000265	
L000266	{\color{Blue4}\textit{Scalar Spin Chirality.---}}
L000267	
L000268	While a magnetic Zeeman field \emph{explicitly} breaks the $S=1/2$ time-reversal symmetry $\mathcal{T} = e^{i \pi S^y} \mathcal{K}$ (here, $\mathcal{K}$ denotes complex conjugation), the sign of the optimal gauge flux $\phi \neq 0$ is arbitrary, implying that the wavefunctions \emph{spontaneously} break another $\mathbb{Z}_2$ symmetry $\mathcal{T}' = \mathcal{K}$.
L000269	As an order parameter, we examine the scalar chirality
L000270	\begin{equation}
L000271	\chi_{ijk} \equiv \big\langle \vec{S}_i \cdot (\vec{S}_j \times \vec{S}_k) \big\rangle
L000272	\end{equation}
L000273	on elementary triangles, a SU(2)-invariant three-spin observable that in principle can be probed experimentally.
L000274	Figure~\ref{fig:chirality}(a) shows $\chi_{ijk}$ in a representative $|C|=1$ projected Landau-level state, which is staggered on up and down triangles (with a uniform magnitude). For $\phi=0$, by contrast, symmetry enforces $\chi=0$ (cf. Sec.~VIII in \cite{SuppMat}).
L000275	Figure~\ref{fig:chirality}(b) shows
L000276	substantial chirality in the $|C|=1$ Landau-level states that varies smoothly with $m$ and exhibits little size dependence (Fig.~S6 in \cite{SuppMat}).
L000277	Representative $|C|=2$ chirality data are shown in Fig.~S7 in \cite{SuppMat}.
L000278	
L000279	\begin{figure}[t]
L000280	    \centering
L000281	    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{chirality.pdf}
L000282	    \caption{
L000283	    (a) Scalar spin chirality $\chi_{ijk} \equiv \big\langle \vec{S}_i \cdot (\vec{S}_j \times \vec{S}_k) \big\rangle$ on a $6\times 6$ lattice with $|C|=1$ and $m=2/3$.
L000284	(b) Plaquette-averaged magnitude $\overline{|\chi|}$ versus magnetization $m$ for $|C|=1$ on a $36\times 36$ lattice.
L000285	The gray curve shows the classical umbrella-state chirality for reference.
L000286	(c) Ratio $\mathcal{R}$ for $|C|=1$ on the same lattice.
L000287	    }
L000288	    \label{fig:chirality}
L000289	\end{figure}
L000290	
L000291	For comparison, we consider a classical umbrella/cone state of spin-$1/2$ moments,
L000292	with in-plane $120^\circ$ order and finite magnetization due to out-of-plane canting (similar to the projected Landau-level state; see Sec.~IX in \cite{SuppMat}),
L000293	whose chirality is generally lower than in the projected Landau-level state [Fig.~\ref{fig:chirality}(b)].
L000294	To further quantify how ``quantum''
L000295	the chirality
L000296	is, we consider the ratio,
L000297	\begin{align}
L000298	&\mathcal{R} \equiv
L000299	\chi_{ijk}/\Big[\frac{i}{2}\langle S^z_i \rangle  \Big(\big\langle S_j^+S_k^-\big\rangle-\big\langle S_j^-S_k^+\big\rangle \nonumber \\
L000300	&+ \big\langle S_k^+S_i^-\big\rangle-\big\langle S_k^-S_i^+\big\rangle + \big\langle S_i^+S_j^-\big\rangle-\big\langle S_i^-S_j^+\big\rangle\Big)\Big].
L000301	\end{align}
L000302	(see also Sec.~IV in \cite{SuppMat}).
L000303	Figure~\ref{fig:chirality}(c) shows $\mathcal{R}>1$ generically, demonstrating sizable connected three-spin correlations beyond a simple factorization into lower-order expectation values, and highlighting that the scalar chirality is a genuinely quantum, intrinsic property generated by the gauge flux and Gutzwiller projection.
L000304	
L000305	{\color{Blue4}\textit{Spin-Nematic Order for $|C|=2$.---}}
L000306	
L000307	For $|C|=2$, a $2\pi$-flux insertion binds a net total spin $\Delta S^z =2$, as created by an operator of the form $S^+_i S^+_j$, rather than a single $S_i^+$.
L000308	We therefore consider possible $S^+S^+$ (spin-nematic) order characterized by matrix elements $\langle S_i^+  S_j^+ \rangle_\mathrm{mono} \equiv \langle n+1 \vert P\, S_i^+  S_j^+ \, P \vert n \rangle/\langle n \vert P \vert n \rangle$, which can be nonzero for any $i\neq j$.
L000309	Remarkably, we find $|\langle S_i^+  S_j^+ \rangle_\mathrm{mono}|$ is not maximized at short distances: it grows with $|\mathbf{r}_i-\mathbf{r}_j|$ and approaches a nearly distance-independent plateau at large separation (see Fig.~S8 in \cite{SuppMat}).
L000310	To investigate the spatial structure of this order parameter, we focus on the simplest, nearest-neighbor pairs.
L000311	Figure~\ref{fig:bonds_Scaling}(a) shows that the bonds form a three-sublattice pattern with a $\sqrt{3}\times\sqrt{3}$-unit cell, characteristic of $K$-point ordering.
L000312	Consistently, the four-spin correlator $\langle S_i^+  S_j^+ S_k^-  S_l^-\rangle$ exhibits the same structure (see Fig.~S9 in \cite{SuppMat}).
L000313	
L000314	\begin{figure}[t]
L000315	    \centering
L000316	    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{bonds_scaling.pdf}
L000317	    \caption{
L000318	(a) Phases of the nearest-neighbour $\langle S_i^+ S_j^+\rangle_{\mathrm{mono}}$
L000319	relative to the bond $(i,j)=((1,0),(0,0))$, for $|C|=2$ and $m=1/3$.
L000320	(b) Finite-size scaling of the (nearest-neighbour) bond-averaged magnitude $\overline{|\langle S_i^+ S_j^+\rangle_{\mathrm{mono}}|}$, and of the corresponding four-spin correlator
L000321	$\overline{|\langle S_i^+ S_j^+ S_k^- S_l^- + S_i^- S_j^- S_k^+ S_l^+\rangle|}$, which serves as an $N$-normalized structure-factor analogue.
L000322	Dashed lines are linear fits.
L000323	    }
L000324	    \label{fig:bonds_Scaling}
L000325	\end{figure}
L000326	
L000327	We analyze the finite-size scaling of this spin-pair order.
L000328	In Fig.~\ref{fig:bonds_Scaling}(b),
L000329	the two measures are approximately linear in $N^{-1}$ with a positive intercept.
L000330	This may be compatible with long-ranged order, or a slow algebraic decay with a non-integrable tail.
L000331	Our data do not distinguish these possibilities, but in either case the correlations are more extended than short-ranged correlations, which may be related to the aforementioned ``unbounded'' real-space behavior.
L000332	While appearing more challenging to realize than $|C|=1$,
L000333	the projected $|C|=2$ state exhibits spin-nematic order that merits further study.
L000334	
L000335	{\color{Blue4}\textit{Summary and discussion.---}}
L000336	
L000337	We studied a family of Gutzwiller-projected spinon wavefunctions with non-trivial gauge flux as variational states for magnetized DSLs on the triangular lattice.
L000338	The $|C|=1$ Landau-level state is favored among the gapped flux candidates we explored: it has the lowest variational energy and the smallest energy variance in our correlation-matrix reconstruction, suggesting that it lies closest to a quasi-local Heisenberg-type parent Hamiltonian.
L000339	Extensive finite-size scaling analysis points to slowly decaying, likely algebraic, in-plane antiferromagnetic $120^\circ$-type correlations. We further find that the projected wavefunctions feature a staggered scalar spin chirality (on elementary triangles) which may be viewed as an order parameter for a $\mathbb{Z}_2$ symmetry which is broken spontaneously by a finite gauge flux.
L000340	The observed correlations are similar to those of a non-coplanar cone/umbrella state of magnetic moments on the triangular lattice (though we stress that we do not find evidence for genuine long-range antiferromagnetic order), which may be attributed to the presence of a uniform gauge flux -- this is in stark contrast to semiclassical studies of triangular lattice Heisenberg antiferromagnets in a field which consistently find coplanar (non-chiral) Y-states~\cite{ye17,keselman2025}.
L000341	We stress that the studied states with finite $m=1/3$ and $2/3$ do not correspond to the magnetization plateaus, which distinguishes our work from Chern-Simons theories of incompressible magnetization plateaus in the Shastry-Sutherland and kagome models~\cite{PhysRevLett.87.097203,PhysRevB.90.174409}.
L000342	
L000343	Studying projected wavefunctions associated with the $|C|=2$ sector, we observe higher-rank magnetic ordering tendencies, where the leading order parameter is a spin-nematic pattern in $\langle S_i^+ S_j^+\rangle$ with an unusual ``unbounded'' real-space behavior. How such a state might be realized in realistic microscopic models is an interesting question for further studies.
L000344	
L000345	Taken together, our results shed light on properties of a large class of Gutzwiller-projected wavefunctions which support a finite magnetization via the formation of spinon Chern gaps. They may help to motivate and interpret future numerical and experimental searches for the physics of magnetized DSLs, in particular the formation of emergent gauge fluxes, in triangular-lattice materials.
L000346	
L000347	{\color{Blue4}\textit{Note added.---}}During the preparation of this manuscript, an independent study~\cite{bad2025} has reported VMC results for the triangular lattice antiferromagnet in a Zeeman field, where in the vicinity of a zero-field DSL the $|C|=1$ flux state is found to energetically outcompete semiclassical (coplanar) ordered states, and Ref.~\cite{yang26} has studied the competition of field-induced emergent gauge fluxes and ring-exchange terms.
L000348	More recently, a combined variational Monte Carlo and field-theory
L000349	study has reported a finite-flux state with finite scalar chirality at
L000350	small Zeeman fields in the triangular-lattice \(J_1\)-\(J_2\) Heisenberg model~\cite{Budaraju2026monopole}.
