L000376	
L000377	{\color{Blue4}\textit{Gutzwiller-projected wavefunctions.---}}
L000378	
L000379	We construct Gutzwiller-projected wavefunctions via an auxiliary spinon Hamiltonian
L000380	\begin{equation}
L000381	    H = \sum_{\sigma = \uparrow,\downarrow} \sum_{ij} t_{ij} f_{i,\sigma}^\dagger f_{j,\sigma} + \mathrm{h.c.} \label{spinonham}
L000382	\end{equation}
L000383	Here, the $t_{ij}$ are complex hopping amplitudes that define an \emph{ansatz}, and in particular also encode the flux experienced by the spinons. For simplicity, we focus on a nearest-neighbor ansatz and fix the amplitude $|t_{ij}| = |t|$.
L000384	
L000385	We take the staggered-$(\pi,0)$ spinon ansatz as our starting point for the unmagnetized Dirac spin liquid (DSL), i.e., in the absence of a Zeeman field.
L000386	For constructing the Landau-level states, we introduce a uniform orbital magnetic field by attaching additional Peierls phases
L000387	$\varphi_{ij}$ to the nearest-neighbor hoppings on the triangular lattice,
L000388	so that $t_{ij} \to t_{ij} e^{i \varphi_{ij}}$. In this work we define
L000389	$\phi$ as the total U(1) flux through a primitive rhombic unit cell of
L000390	the triangular lattice, i.e.\ the oriented sum of link phases around the
L000391	boundary of any such unit cell satisfies
L000392	\begin{equation}
L000393	    \sum_{\langle ij\rangle \in \lozenge} \varphi_{ij}
L000394	    \;=\; \phi \pmod{2\pi} . \label{sumflux}
L000395	\end{equation}
L000396	Since each primitive rhombic unit cell contains two elementary
L000397	triangular plaquettes, the corresponding flux per triangle is $\Phi \equiv \phi/2$.
L000398	
L000399	The free-fermion Hamiltonian $H$ is readily diagonalized for a system of $N$ sites, yielding $2 N$ single-particle wavefunctions.
L000400	A Slater determinant $\ket{\psi_f}$ at magnetization $M$ is obtained by filling the $N_\uparrow = (N+M)/2$ and $N_\downarrow = (N-M)/2$ lowest-energy orbitals for spin-up and -down spinons, respectively.
L000401	Therefore, we occupy $N$ spinon states in total -- i.e., half filling (one spinon per site).
L000402	Equivalently, the same filling can be obtained by adding a spinon Zeeman term
L000403	\begin{equation}
L000404	    H \to H -h_z \sum_i \left(f_{i,\uparrow}^{\dagger}f_{i,\uparrow}
L000405	    - f_{i,\downarrow}^{\dagger}f_{i,\downarrow}\right)
L000406	\end{equation}
L000407	to the Hamiltonian in Eq.~\eqref{spinonham} and filling the lowest $N$ spinon orbitals.
L000408	We further define  $m = M/N$ as the intensive magnetization. Unless stated otherwise, magnetization values are reported in terms of $m$.
L000409	
L000410	
L000411	At fixed $m$ and flux $\phi$,
L000412	when the spinon Fermi level lies in a well-defined Chern gap, we label the corresponding flux state by the gap Chern number.
L000413	For $m>0$, the spin-up and spin-down Fermi levels lie in particle-hole-related gaps with opposite Chern numbers: a $|C|=1$ state has them in $C=+1$ and $C=-1$ gaps, while $|C|=2$ is defined analogously.
L000414	Representative cases used in the main text are illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:em_C_state_construction}.
L000415	In the highlighted cases, both spin-resolved Fermi levels lie in single-particle Chern gaps, so the occupied spinon levels form closed shells.
L000416	
L000417	
L000418	\begin{figure}[t]
L000419	    \centering
L000420	    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{suppfig_C_state_construction.pdf}
L000421	    \caption{
L000422	Construction of the Chern spinon states.
L000423	(a) Hofstadter spectrum and (b)
L000424	Corresponding Wannier diagram, where the vertical axis denotes the normalized state index (cumulative filling) $y$ and the color scale encodes the size of the energy gap between adjacent levels.
L000425	For rational flux $\phi/2\pi$, a tracked gap follows $y=C\,(\phi/2\pi)+s$, so the slope gives the gap Chern number $C$.
L000426	(c,d) Representative spinon spectra at $\phi=\pi/3$ and $2\pi/3$ in a convenient gauge, showing nearly flat Landau-level-like bands separated by Chern gaps.
L000427	Green (purple) annotations denote band (gap) Chern numbers.
L000428	The momentum labels in (c,d) are dimensionless primitive-lattice phase coordinates
L000429	$(k_1,k_2)\equiv(\mathbf{k}\!\cdot\!\mathbf a_1,\mathbf{k}\!\cdot\!\mathbf a_2)$, not Cartesian momentum components.
L000430	Gold circles, gold squares, and pink hexagons mark the spin-up/down Fermi levels for the $|C|=1$ state at $m=1/3$, the $|C|=1$ state at $m=2/3$, and the $|C|=2$ state at $m=1/3$, respectively; in (c,d), the corresponding horizontal guide lines are dashed, dash-dotted, and dotted, respectively.
L000431	}
L000432	\label{fig:em_C_state_construction}
L000433	\end{figure}
L000434	
L000435	
L000436	A variational wavefunction for the spin-$1/2$ moments on the triangular lattice is obtained by projecting the Slater determinant $\ket{\psi_f}$ onto the subspace of single-occupied sites, $f_{i,\uparrow}^\dagger f_{i,\uparrow} + f_{i,\downarrow}^\dagger f_{i,\downarrow} = 1 \ \forall i$.
L000437	This projection leaves $N_\uparrow$ and $N_\downarrow$ unchanged, so the magnetization $M$ is preserved.
L000438	While explicitly constructing (and storing) the projected wavefunction $P \ket{\psi_f}$ is only feasible for small systems, \emph{observables} with respect to $P \ket{\psi_f}$ can be efficiently evaluated using Monte Carlo methods~\cite{BeccaSorella2017}.
