L000564	\section{Implementing Overall Flux}
L000565	
L000566	There are different ways to assign Peierls phases \(\varphi_{ij}\) to the bonds.
L000567	For a fixed flux pattern, different assignments correspond to different gauge choices of the vector potential \(\mathbf{A}\), and are therefore physically equivalent: they yield the same accumulated phase around any closed loop.
L000568	A convenient general construction is
L000569	\begin{equation}
L000570	\varphi_{ij}=\int_{\mathbf{r}_i}^{\mathbf{r}_j}\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r})\cdot d\bm{\ell},
L000571	\label{eq:peierls-phase}
L000572	\end{equation}
L000573	where \(\mathbf{r}_i=(r_{ix},r_{iy})\) is the position of site \(i\), and the line integral is taken along the straight-line segment connecting \(\mathbf{r}_i\) and \(\mathbf{r}_j\).
L000574	For simplicity, we work in units where we absorb the factor \(e/\hbar\) into \(\mathbf{A}\).
L000575	We choose a vector potential \(\mathbf{A}\) of the form
L000576	\begin{equation}
L000577	\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r})=B
L000578	\begin{bmatrix}
L000579	-\alpha r_y\\
L000580	(1-\alpha) r_x
L000581	\end{bmatrix}, \label{vectorpotential}
L000582	\end{equation}
L000583	where \(\alpha\in\mathbb{R}\) parametrizes a family of gauges that all produce the same uniform magnetic field.
L000584	To verify this, note that in two spatial directions the out-of-plane curl is
L000585	\begin{equation}
L000586	(\nabla\times\mathbf{A})_z=\partial_x A_y-\partial_y A_x
L000587	=B,
L000588	\end{equation}
L000589	i.e.
L000590	\begin{equation}
L000591	\nabla\times \mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r})=B\,\hat{\mathbf z}.
L000592	\label{eq:curlA}
L000593	\end{equation}
L000594	Using Stokes' theorem, the left-hand side of Eq.~(9) in the End Matter can be written as a line integral of \(\mathbf{A}\),
L000595	\begin{equation}
L000596	\sum_{\langle ij\rangle \in \lozenge} \varphi_{ij}
L000597	\;=\;\oint_{\partial\lozenge}\mathbf{A}\cdot d\bm{\ell}
L000598	\;=\;\iint_{\lozenge} (\nabla\times \mathbf{A})\cdot \hat{\mathbf z}\, d^2 r
L000599	\;=\; B\,A_{\rm uc},
L000600	\end{equation}
L000601	so that the flux through the primitive rhombic unit cell is
L000602	\begin{equation}
L000603	\phi \equiv B\,A_{\rm uc}\pmod{2\pi}.
L000604	\label{eq:phi_equals_BA}
L000605	\end{equation}
L000606	For a triangular lattice with lattice constant set to \(a=1\), the area of the primitive rhombus is
L000607	$A_{\rm uc}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.
L000608	
L000609	On a finite cluster with periodic boundary conditions (a torus), the total U(1) flux piercing the system must be quantized in units of \(2\pi\). The system contains \(N\) primitive unit cells, so
L000610	\begin{equation}
L000611	N\,\phi = 2\pi n,\qquad n\in\mathbb{Z},
L000612	\label{eq:total_flux_quantization}
L000613	\end{equation}
L000614	which implies that the flux per primitive unit cell can only take the discrete values
L000615	\begin{equation}
L000616	\phi=\frac{2\pi}{N}\,n,\qquad n=0,1,\dots,N-1.
L000617	\label{eq:phi_discrete}
L000618	\end{equation}
L000619	Combining Eqs.~\eqref{eq:phi_discrete} and \eqref{eq:phi_equals_BA} then gives the corresponding allowed values of the uniform field strength,
L000620	\begin{equation}
L000621	B_n=\frac{\phi}{A_{\rm uc}}
L000622	=\frac{4\pi n}{\sqrt{3}\,N}.
L000623	\label{eq:B_discrete}
L000624	\end{equation}
L000625	
L000626	
L000627	When a nearest-neighbor bond crosses the boundary of a finite cluster with periodic identification, one endpoint of the bond lies outside the chosen fundamental domain. We then map that site back into the cluster by a Bravais translation \(\mathbf{L}\). Because our \(\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r})\) in Eq.~\eqref{vectorpotential} is not periodic, this ``folding back'' generally produces an extra (gauge) phase. Instead of the ordinary boundary conditions, we need a modified boundary condition to ensure that the Hamiltonian on the torus is single-valued and that the prescribed flux (i.e., the accumulated Peierls phase around any closed loop) is unchanged by the choice of representative sites.
L000628	
L000629	From Eq.~\eqref{vectorpotential},
L000630	\begin{equation}
L000631	\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r}+\mathbf{L})-\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r})=\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{L}),
L000632	\end{equation}
L000633	and the right-hand side is a constant vector, which can be written as the gradient of a scalar function,
L000634	\begin{equation}
L000635	\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r}+\mathbf{L})=\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r})+\nabla\chi_{\mathbf{L}}(\mathbf{r}),
L000636	\qquad
L000637	\chi_{\mathbf{L}}(\mathbf{r})=\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{L})\cdot \mathbf{r} .
L000638	\label{eq:A_quasiperiodic}
L000639	\end{equation}
L000640	Using Eq.~\eqref{eq:peierls-phase}, shifting both endpoints by \(\mathbf{L}\) gives
L000641	\begin{align}
L000642	\varphi_{i+\mathbf{L},\,j+\mathbf{L}}
L000643	&=\int_{\mathbf{r}_i+\mathbf{L}}^{\mathbf{r}_j+\mathbf{L}}\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r})\cdot d\bm{\ell}
L000644	=\int_{\mathbf{r}_i}^{\mathbf{r}_j}\big[\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r}+\mathbf{L})\big]\cdot d\bm{\ell} =\int_{\mathbf{r}_i}^{\mathbf{r}_j}\big[\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r})+\nabla\chi_{\mathbf{L}}(\mathbf{r})\big]\cdot d\bm{\ell} \nonumber \\
L000645	&=\varphi_{ij}+\chi_{\mathbf{L}}(\mathbf{r}_j)-\chi_{\mathbf{L}}(\mathbf{r}_i)
L000646	\quad (\mathrm{mod}\;2\pi).
L000647	\label{eq:phi_translate}
L000648	\end{align}
L000649	
L000650	On a torus we identify \(\,i\equiv i+\mathbf{L}\). Accordingly, the hopping operator associated with a given physical bond must be independent of the chosen representative sites:
L000651	\begin{equation}
L000652	t \, e^{i\varphi_{ij}}\,c_i^\dagger c_j
L000653	\;\equiv\;
L000654	t \, e^{i\varphi_{i+\mathbf{L},\,j+\mathbf{L}}}\,c_{i+\mathbf{L}}^\dagger c_{j+\mathbf{L}}.
L000655	\label{eq:hopping_invariance}
L000656	\end{equation}
L000657	Substituting Eq.~\eqref{eq:phi_translate} into Eq.~\eqref{eq:hopping_invariance} shows that the extra phase
L000658	\(\chi_{\mathbf{L}}(\mathbf{r}_j)-\chi_{\mathbf{L}}(\mathbf{r}_i)\) must be absorbed by the fermion operators at the boundary. This leads to the modified boundary condition,
L000659	\begin{equation}
L000660	c_{i+\mathbf{L}}=e^{-i\chi_{\mathbf{L}}(\mathbf{r}_i)}\,c_i,
L000661	\qquad
L000662	\chi_{\mathbf{L}}(\mathbf{r})=\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{L})\cdot \mathbf{r} .
L000663	\label{eq:magnetic_bc}
L000664	\end{equation}
L000665	Notice that from Eq.~\eqref{eq:magnetic_bc} one obtains, by replacing \(i\to i-\mathbf L\),
L000666	\begin{equation}
L000667	c_i = e^{-i\chi_{\mathbf L}(\mathbf r_{i-\mathbf L})}\,c_{i-\mathbf L}
L000668	\qquad\Rightarrow\qquad
L000669	c_{i-\mathbf L} = e^{+i\chi_{\mathbf L}(\mathbf r_{i-\mathbf L})}\,c_i .
L000670	\label{eq:bc_minusL_from_plusL_shift}
L000671	\end{equation}
L000672	If one instead writes a separate boundary relation for \(-\mathbf L\),
L000673	\begin{equation}
L000674	c_{i-\mathbf L}=e^{-i\chi_{-\mathbf L}(\mathbf r_i)}\,c_i,
L000675	\qquad \chi_{-\mathbf L}(\mathbf r)=\mathbf{A}(-\mathbf L)\cdot\mathbf r .
L000676	\label{eq:bc_minusL_direct}
L000677	\end{equation}
L000678	Using \(\mathbf r_{i-\mathbf L}=\mathbf r_i-\mathbf L\) and \(\mathbf{A}(-\mathbf L)=-\mathbf{A}(\mathbf L)\), we find
L000679	\begin{equation}
L000680	e^{+i\chi_{\mathbf L}(\mathbf r_{i-\mathbf L})}
L000681	=
L000682	e^{+i\mathbf{A}(\mathbf L)\cdot(\mathbf r_i-\mathbf L)}
L000683	=
L000684	e^{+i\mathbf{A}(\mathbf L)\cdot\mathbf r_i}\,e^{-i\mathbf{A}(\mathbf L)\cdot\mathbf L},
L000685	\end{equation}
L000686	whereas
L000687	\begin{equation}
L000688	e^{-i\chi_{-\mathbf L}(\mathbf r_i)}
L000689	=
L000690	e^{-i\mathbf{A}(-\mathbf L)\cdot\mathbf r_i}
L000691	=
L000692	e^{+i\mathbf{A}(\mathbf L)\cdot\mathbf r_i}.
L000693	\end{equation}
L000694	Thus, Eq.~\eqref{eq:bc_minusL_from_plusL_shift} and Eq.~\eqref{eq:bc_minusL_direct} differ by a constant factor
L000695	\(\exp[-i\mathbf{A}(\mathbf L)\cdot\mathbf L]\) and correspond to different conventions.
L000696	
L000697	It is convenient to write Eq.~\eqref{eq:magnetic_bc} in a symmetric ``midpoint'' form:
L000698	\begin{equation}
L000699	c_i
L000700	=
L000701	c_{i+\mathbf{L}}\exp\!\left(i\,\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{L})\cdot\frac{\mathbf{r}_i+\mathbf{r}_{i+\mathbf{L}}}{2}\right),
L000702	\label{eq:magnetic_bc_midpoint}
L000703	\end{equation}
L000704	which differs from Eq.~\eqref{eq:magnetic_bc} only by an \(i\)-independent constant phase factor
L000705	\(\exp\!\big(\tfrac{i}{2}\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{L})\cdot \mathbf{L}\big)\) (since \(\mathbf{r}_{i+\mathbf{L}}=\mathbf{r}_i+\mathbf{L}\)).
L000706	This constant does not affect any local gauge-invariant quantity. The practical advantage of this convention is that it treats \(\pm\mathbf{L}\) in a manifestly symmetric way, reducing sign mistakes when implementing the folding-back procedure for bonds that cross the boundary in either direction.
L000707	Specifically, from Eq.~\eqref{eq:magnetic_bc_midpoint}, by replacing \(i\to i-\mathbf L\),
L000708	\begin{equation}
L000709	c_{i-\mathbf L}
L000710	=
L000711	c_{i}\exp\!\left(i\,\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{L})\cdot\frac{\mathbf{r}_{i-\mathbf L}+\mathbf{r}_{i}}{2}\right).
L000712	\end{equation}
L000713	Therefore, together with Eq.~\eqref{eq:magnetic_bc_midpoint}, we write
L000714	\begin{equation}
L000715	c_{i}
L000716	=
L000717	c_{i \pm \mathbf L}\exp\!\left(i\,\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{\pm L})\cdot\frac{\mathbf{r}_{i}+\mathbf{r}_{i \pm\mathbf L}}{2}\right),
L000718	\label{eq:midpoint_minusL}
L000719	\end{equation}
L000720	which is convenient and avoids bookkeeping ambiguities when folding sites back into the cluster.
